    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Could you expand a bit more on the actual mechanism within the cells? Specifically, how does the cell wall rupture lead to that sudden, almost \"sneaky\" release of the gas? It seems like it happens so fast as soon as the knife touches it.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. When you cut an onion, the force applied quickly causes the cell walls to rupture, resulting in a sudden and almost instantaneous release of the cellular contents, including enzymes like alliinase. This rupture and gas release are due to the structural integrity of the onion cell being compromised by the mechanical chopping action. Within minutes of cutting, the cell contents, which were previously contained, begin to diffuse out rapidly, often before it is noticeable. Due to the thin oxygen layer over the exposed plant material, the volatilization of gases completes much quicker as the oxygen reacts with ions like protons, carrying the gases away. This process is quite swift, which accounts for the sudden sensation felt in the eyes after minimal contact with onion residue.",
